  "live_records": {
    "q-36992": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36992",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})-\\sqrt{x+1},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz})-\\sqrt{x+1} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{yz}) & =\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{yz} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}} \\\\ yz & ={({e}^{\\sqrt{x+1}})}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{x+1}}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the surface $z=\\frac{{e}^{2\\sqrt{x+1}}}{y}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
