    "q-129184": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129184",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ 2z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the paraboloid $z=\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-129184-a",
