    "q-71584": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71584",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ c(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =1 \\\\ 3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)$ can't be zero or negative. Therefore, we get a family of surfaces given by\n\n$$\n3c{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}=1,\\,c\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
