      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=xy+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z$ that passes through the point $P(0,1,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,1) & =0\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ xy+\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-xy \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-xy) \\\\ z & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(0,1,1)$ is the surface $z=\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
