    "q-71594": {
      "dom_id": "step-q71594",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}$ that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\sqrt{5},3) & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2(2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}-(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3)$ is the surface ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
